Seed: credential flags, target database in output, single-account rename

`npm run seed` silently ignored SEED_EMAIL/SEED_PASSWORD when the variables
did not reach the process, handing back the default account with a generated
password and no clue which database had just been changed.

- Accepts `--email` / `--password` flags, which survive shell differences
  better than environment variables (env vars still work; flags win).
- Prints the database it wrote to, saying PRODUCTION (Neon) explicitly. With
  local and production sharing these scripts, seeding the wrong one was
  invisible.
- Supplying a different email now renames the single account. `upsert` keyed
  on email would have created a second one, breaking the single-account rule
  (D19).
- Reports what it did: created, password reset, or renamed from which email.

Verified against a throwaway database: all three credential paths, exactly
one account remaining afterwards, the new password authenticating and the
old one rejected.

